  "context": [
    "MG entered India in 2019 and the Hector was its first product, built on a stretched version of the Baojun/Wuling architecture with a heavy emphasis on in-car connectivity, a large touchscreen, and segment-leading feature counts. That gadget-first positioning won early attention but a newer, less-proven badge means resale buyers discount it more heavily than they would an equivalent Hyundai or Tata product.",
    "The current Hector line-up spans a 1.5-litre turbo-petrol and a 2.0-litre diesel, with the diesel still dominating highway-driven, high-km Hectors and the petrol more common on city-first, lower-mileage cars.",
    "As a newer, China-linked brand without the multi-decade dealer trust Maruti, Hyundai, or Toyota enjoy in India, MG's depreciation curve is steeper than segment peers like the Tata Harrier, particularly past the three-year mark when extended-warranty coverage lapses and buyers become more price-sensitive about future parts support.",
    "Early software and infotainment glitches reported on 2019-2021 units, since resolved via updates, still linger in buyer perception and widen price negotiation on older examples, even when the issues have been fully patched at the dealership."
  ],
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is the resale value of an MG Hector in India?",
      "a": "An MG Hector retains roughly 66% of its ex-showroom price after two years and around 56% after three years, dropping to about 42% by year five, as of September 2026 transaction data. A 2022 Sharp diesel with 25,000-50,000 km currently trades in the low-to-mid ₹11-12 lakh range."
    },
    {
      "q": "Is diesel or petrol better for Hector resale?",
      "a": "The diesel remains the most liquid used-market option because it dominates the high-km, long-distance segment of Hector buyers, while the turbo-petrol holds up well on lower-mileage, city-first cars."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does the Hector depreciate faster than a Tata Harrier?",
      "a": "The Hector's 5-year retention of about 42% sits roughly 5 points below the Large SUV segment average of 47%, largely because MG is a newer entrant in India and buyers apply a bigger resale discount for brand and long-term parts-support uncertainty than they do for Tata."
    },
    {
      "q": "How much does a Hector depreciate per year?",
      "a": "A Hector typically loses about 21% of value in year one, settling into 7-9 percentage point annual retention drops through year five (to roughly 42%), and flattening gradually from there to around 21% retention at 10 years."
    },
    {
      "q": "Which city gives the best resale price for a Hector?",
      "a": "Bengaluru trades around +2% above the national mid, helped by strong demand for connected-feature SUVs among tech-sector buyers, while Kolkata trades roughly -3% due to the thinnest MG resale liquidity among major metros."
    }
  ],
